WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.520
Long-time comic book fans will know that, in the&nbsp;
world of superheroes, things are rarely what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.080
seem — and sometimes you really can't trust&nbsp;
what you're being told. Indeed, on a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.920
of occasions, Marvel has pulled the rug out&nbsp;
from under the fans, for better or for worse.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.160
Infinity War was arguably the most&nbsp;
hotly-anticipated instalment of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.720
MCU's third phase, and excitement reached&nbsp;
a fever pitch when that first trailer was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.680
released — which ended with a climactic shot of&nbsp;
Cap leading the Avengers into battle. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.440
at the time, it made perfect sense&nbsp;
that Hulk would be among them, too.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.840
The only problem? Hulk is nowhere to&nbsp;
be found for most of Infinity War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.840
let alone the battle sequences shown in&nbsp;
the trailer. At the start of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.840
Hulk suffers an embarrassing defeat&nbsp;
during a head to head battle with Thanos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.840
and Bruce Banner is unable to revert&nbsp;
back into his giant green form.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.200
"Dude, you're embarrassing&nbsp;
me in front of the wizards."

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.200
"I'm sorry, I can't, or he won't."

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.560
"It's alright. Hey, stand down.&nbsp;
Keep an eye on him, thank you."

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.120
In the end, Bruce dons the Hulkbuster&nbsp;
suit during the final battle against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.360
Thanos' forces, and the trailer's slo-mo&nbsp;
ensemble shot is nowhere to be found.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
So why the deception? Well, the Russo&nbsp;
brothers have said in interviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.360
that they purposefully crafted the&nbsp;
trailers of their Avengers films to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.720
minimize spoilers and keep fans guessing.&nbsp;
This included altering smaller details like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.960
Thor's eyepatch and certain missing&nbsp;
Infinity Stones on Thanos' gauntlet.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.773
Inevitably, the Russo brothers tricked the fans&nbsp;
again with the trailers leading up to Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.773 --> 00:01:34.240
Endgame — but this time around, most people kinda&nbsp;
expected it. Considering Endgame might well have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.120
been the most eagerly-awaited major movie release&nbsp;
of the last few decades, you can hardly blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.840
the Russos for wanting to keep a tight lid on&nbsp;
what plot developments were going to unfold.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.080
That said, it's still impressive how little&nbsp;
those trailers gave away about the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.160
Most of the shots featured in the Endgame&nbsp;
trailers utilize footage from either the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.920
very first scenes of the movie — or a&nbsp;
few choice shots of the final battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.800
The Russos' conservative use of scenes made&nbsp;
sense, however, since using unaltered footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.680
from the film's latter two acts would have&nbsp;
likely spoiled the movie's time heist premise.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.960
One of the most noticeable&nbsp;
misdirections in the Endgame trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.240
was Black Widow sporting her Infinity War-era bob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.040
when in fact she'd gone back to her natural&nbsp;
red color. There's also a shot of Thor wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.840
Stormbreaker — and the fact he looks a whole&nbsp;
lot slimmer than he does in the actual movie.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.360
Marvel's history of Avengers-level deceit isn't&nbsp;
exclusive to the Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.320
though. Over the years, avid Marvel fans have&nbsp;
been lied to countless times through countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.080
misleading storylines and retcons that completely&nbsp;
flip previously established canon on its head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.760
One such retcon involved Captain America's tragic&nbsp;
passing after the Civil War crossover event.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.120
At the heart of Civil War's seven-issue conflict&nbsp;
is the Superhero Registration Act, which requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.680
anyone with superhuman abilities to trust the&nbsp;
U.S. government with their secret identities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.880
and regulate the presence and actions of heroes.&nbsp;
Cap is against the Superhero Registration Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.840
of course, which he sees as a grave infringement&nbsp;
on the rights of people with superhuman abilities;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.480
one of his allies, Luke Cage, even sees&nbsp;
the act as akin to slavery. During the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.920
dramatic fallout of Civil War, Steve Rogers is&nbsp;
assassinated by a brainwashed Sharon Carter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.720
This sets up the premise for The Death of&nbsp;
Captain America, which spans 18 issues.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.280
Rogers' death had wide-reaching implications&nbsp;
for the Marvel universe, and eventually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.080
title of Captain America was passed down&nbsp;
to Bucky Barnes. But these are comic books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.720
and apparently no one ever stays dead.&nbsp;
Inevitably, Marvel brought Cap back by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.720
saying he hadn't actually died — the&nbsp;
gun he was shot with didn't kill him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.400
but rather suspended him in time and space. It was&nbsp;
a nice excuse to bring back a fan-favorite, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.040
also kind of drains all the emotional weight from&nbsp;
The Death of Captain America, don't you think?

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.120
One of the more egregious retcons to plague the&nbsp;
Avengers, The Crossing's reveal that Tony Stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.240
has been a traitorous sleeper agent for Kang the&nbsp;
Conqueror for 30 years went against literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.880
everything readers knew about Iron Man. While&nbsp;
retconning heroes as villains for a short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.400
narrative arc is commonplace in the comics, this&nbsp;
instance was especially unpopular among fans.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.760
Unlike the other alternate-universe-evil-twin&nbsp;
plots that comic book fans had come to expect over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.120
the years, The Crossing saw the real Tony Stark&nbsp;
readers knew and loved betraying his friends — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.080
straight-up killing people — after three decades&nbsp;
of deception. The explanation that Kang the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.880
Conqueror had been manipulating Tony for decades&nbsp;
seemed a little thin, not only because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.320
rich backstory that had been thoroughly explored&nbsp;
during years of Iron Man comics, but also because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.200
it completely disregarded any sense readers had&nbsp;
of Tony's character development or morality.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:46.080
Except… not really. Marvel quickly backpedaled&nbsp;
on the idea that Tony Stark had been manipulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.280
into being an evil double agent by Kang for&nbsp;
the entire history of the Marvel Universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.000
Instead, the Avengers Forever miniseries&nbsp;
pulled off a double-retcon that explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.600
that the manipulation of Tony had&nbsp;
taken place over several months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.760
not years, which only served to confuse&nbsp;
and frustrate readers even more.

00:05:05.922 --> 00:05:10.058
"Hail Hydra"

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.040
Arguably the most controversial&nbsp;
hero-to-villain retcon in the Marvel Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.600
began with a now-infamous cliffhanger&nbsp;
in Captain America: Steve Rogers #1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.480
that sees Cap pledging his allegiance to&nbsp;
Hydra. Throughout the Secret Empire storyline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.200
it's revealed that Rogers was indoctrinated&nbsp;
as a double agent for Hydra since childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.720
and had been plotting against the&nbsp;
Allied powers since the start.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.480
The idea that Captain America, a historically&nbsp;
anti-Nazi hero created by Jewish creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:44.320
truly sided with Nazi-adjacent organization Hydra&nbsp;
did not sit well with readers to say the least.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:48.320
Some understandably saw it as downright&nbsp;
offensive to Captain America's original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.040
creators and the real-life communities&nbsp;
that had been irreparably harmed by Nazism.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.840
In an attempt at damage control, Marvel tried&nbsp;
to argue that Hydra wasn't an explicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.400
Nazi organization, and that Cap's "Hail&nbsp;
Hydra" moment wouldn't be trivialized with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:06.800
a bait-and-switch narrative involving&nbsp;
alternate timeline versions of Rogers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:12.000
Critics argued, however, that while Hydra's&nbsp;
origins might have predated Nazism in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.960
their ideologies had since become intertwined.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.800
Now, the truth behind Cap's secret Hydra&nbsp;
allegiance is a little more complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:23.920
than it first appears, with the Cosmic Cube&nbsp;
altering both reality and Steve's memories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.880
Still, Secret Empire rewrites history and&nbsp;
says that the hero Captain America readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:34.080
thought they knew was the biggest lie of all,&nbsp;
and that HydraCap is Rogers' original form.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.400
At the end of Secret Empire, the story&nbsp;
climaxes in a battle between different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.040
versions of Steve Rogers. The heroic Steve wins&nbsp;
out, of course, but that didn't change much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.840
of what had happened — and the whole storyline&nbsp;
still stung for plenty of Captain America fans.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.840
In Black Widow's first origin story,&nbsp;
Natasha Romanoff danced as a ballerina&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.480
for the Russian Bolshoi before her time&nbsp;
as a KGB spy-turned-Avenger. For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.640
earliest Black Widow storylines, readers&nbsp;
learned that she married a Soviet agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.280
and carried on as a prominent ballerina,&nbsp;
but later pledged her loyalty to the KGB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.680
and trained as a spy after the death of&nbsp;
her husband. Black Widow's dancer past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.080
was even alluded to in the MCU during&nbsp;
scenes from Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:21.040
But the truth behind Black Widow's backstory&nbsp;
was far more sinister than either the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.400
tragic origins or its MCU interpretation.&nbsp;
In the comics, the reality of Black Widow's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.280
past is that Natasha was brainwashed,&nbsp;
trained, and bio-technically altered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.240
be a cold-blooded assassin from childhood&nbsp;
by a Soviet program known as the Red Room.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.480
Fans pointed out that being a member of an&nbsp;
internationally recognized dance company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.040
might make it difficult to moonlight as an&nbsp;
assassin, but this was easily explained away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:50.320
by saying that all of Natasha's memories of the&nbsp;
ballet had never happened. Instead, the false&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.600
narrative Natasha initially believed about her&nbsp;
past was planted by the Red Room to ensure her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.960
loyalty to the KGB. Remember this particular&nbsp;
deception, too, because the lies Natasha was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.440
told about her past will likely be crucial to&nbsp;
the plot of the upcoming Black Widow movie.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.360
Scarlet Witch's backstory has been retconned&nbsp;
almost too many times to count. An integral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.640
part of Wanda's origin — one that people took for&nbsp;
granted for decades — was that she and her brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.280
Quicksilver were Magneto's children, their&nbsp;
mutant powers being inherited through birth.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.360
As Scarlet Witch would later star as an&nbsp;
Avenger in the Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.640
this posed problems for Disney and Marvel — since,&nbsp;
at the time, Fox owned the rights to the X-Men and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.840
most mutant characters. When Marvel fans were&nbsp;
introduced to Wanda in Avengers: Age of Ultron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.400
her powers are shown to be the result of&nbsp;
experiments by Hydra, not mutant genes.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.920
"There's nothing more horrifying… than a miracle."

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.600
Then, in 2014, Marvel turned Scarlet&nbsp;
Witch's origin story on its head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:59.520
During the AXIS comics crossover event, Wanda&nbsp;
discovers she's not actually Magneto's daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.720
rewriting the fraught birth and childhood of the&nbsp;
twins once again. This retcon also revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.680
readers had been lied to about the origins of&nbsp;
Scarlet Witch's powers. She had previously been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.160
depicted as having some magical powers along&nbsp;
with her mutant abilities, but the reveal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.440
her biological connection to Magneto was a&nbsp;
lie reinforced Marvel's growing emphasis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.320
her sorcery. Essentially, Marvel took all the&nbsp;
mutant out of one of its most famous mutants.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.920
Back in the first Guardians of the Galaxy&nbsp;
movie, Thanos' adopted daughter Gamora&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.800
is introduced as the last member of a species&nbsp;
called the Zehoberei. Now, many fans might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.200
remember that specific detail — and that's&nbsp;
probably just what Marvel is hoping for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.120
because they later took her origin story&nbsp;
in an entirely different direction.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.600
As explained in Infinity War, Thanos was&nbsp;
indeed responsible for the destruction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.440
her people — but it doesn't seem like he&nbsp;
actually slaughtered all of them, as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.960
claimed in Guardians. Instead, Thanos is shown&nbsp;
in a flashback sequence, making Gamora's planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:02.400
a stop on his long quest to bring "balance" to the&nbsp;
universe, by wiping out half of all life there.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.560
Of course, it's entirely possible that the&nbsp;
rest of the Zehoberei had perished between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:11.120
the events of the Infinity War flashback and&nbsp;
Guardians of the Galaxy. If that's the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.680
however, there hasn't been any explicit&nbsp;
on-screen explanation to that effect.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.480
Either way, Gamora's story is tragic, and she&nbsp;
had plenty of reasons to want Thanos stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.280
at all costs — but this seems like one of those&nbsp;
rare occasions when Marvel hadn't really figured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:30.160
out the specific details of its sprawling&nbsp;
cinematic universe. It doesn't happen often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.040
but it appears that, in this instance, Feige&nbsp;
and his creative team made changes on the fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.880
hoping most members of the audience would&nbsp;
be too caught up in the action to notice.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.520
Avengers: Age of Ultron isn't generally regarded&nbsp;
as one of the best entries in the MCU, but it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.800
its strong points — including the closing moments&nbsp;
of the film, when the Hulk commandeers an Avengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.000
Quinjet and heads off for parts unknown. He&nbsp;
later turns up on Sakaar in Thor: Ragnarok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.360
as a sort of gladiatorial all-star who battles new&nbsp;
contenders for the amusement of the Grandmaster.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:06.800
It's basically the MCU's version of the&nbsp;
classic Planet Hulk storyline from the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:11.920
and a turn of events that fans had long been&nbsp;
clamoring for. It also wasn't initially supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.200
to be that way. As Marvel Studios chief Kevin&nbsp;
Feige later admitted, when Ultron was made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.000
the MCU braintrust had no idea where Hulk&nbsp;
would end up — but they explicitly told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:27.200
Age of Ultron director Joss Whedon that he&nbsp;
wouldn't be going to space. Feige explains:

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:29.440
"If you go back and look&nbsp;
at that shot of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.560
he's in the sky. It's blue&nbsp;
sky. In the movie, it's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:37.760
'We lost the signal out by the Caspian Sea.'&nbsp;
Or something like that, it was all earthbound."

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.160
Here's one case in which it seems safe to assume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:44.880
that most Marvel fans can forgive the studio&nbsp;
for a little shift in direction — after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:49.360
their change of heart gave us Thor: Ragnarok.&nbsp;
And who could possibly argue with that?

